ACC-009 — Engineering Knowledge

Document ID: ACC-009
Title: Engineering Knowledge
Version: 1.0.0-draft
Status: Draft

1. Purpose

This specification defines Engineering Knowledge, the stable and reusable body of validated engineering understanding maintained within the Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework (AECF).

Engineering Knowledge represents engineering understanding that has demonstrated sufficient validity, consistency, and applicability to be reused across multiple engineering contexts while remaining subject to future refinement.

2. Scope

This specification applies to all Engineering Knowledge maintained, shared, or consumed within Atlas.

Engineering Knowledge may include:

Engineering principles
Design patterns
Engineering rules
Best practices
Engineering methods
Validated models
Domain relationships
Engineering heuristics
Reusable engineering solutions
Lessons learned

Engineering Knowledge is independent of any single project, observation, or reasoning session.

3. Canonical Definition

Engineering Knowledge is stable, validated, and reusable engineering understanding that has been preserved through repeated verification and is applicable across one or more engineering contexts.

Engineering Knowledge is not simply stored information.

It is understanding that has matured through continued validation and practical applicability.

4. Characteristics

Engineering Knowledge SHALL possess the following characteristics.

4.1 Validated

Engineering Knowledge SHALL be supported by validated Engineering Understanding.

4.2 Reusable

Engineering Knowledge SHALL be applicable beyond the context in which it was originally developed.

4.3 Stable

Engineering Knowledge SHALL exhibit relative stability while remaining open to refinement as new evidence emerges.

4.4 Traceable

Engineering Knowledge SHALL maintain traceability to the Engineering Understanding and Engineering Evidence from which it originated.

4.5 Explainable

Engineering Knowledge SHALL support transparent explanation of its origin, rationale, and applicability.

4.6 Generalized

Engineering Knowledge SHALL abstract reusable engineering concepts from specific engineering situations.

4.7 Evolvable

Engineering Knowledge MAY evolve when substantial new Engineering Evidence or Engineering Understanding justifies revision.

5. Relationships

Engineering Knowledge emerges from validated Engineering Understanding preserved through Engineering Memory.

Engineering Understanding
        │
        ▼
Engineering Memory
        │
        ▼
Engineering Knowledge
        │
        ▼
Engineering Reasoning
        │
        ▼
Decision Support

Engineering Knowledge:

Originates from Engineering Understanding.
Is preserved through Engineering Memory.
Supports Engineering Reasoning.
Informs future Engineering Understanding.
6. Responsibilities

Engineering Knowledge is responsible for:

Preserving validated engineering concepts.
Supporting reusable engineering reasoning.
Providing consistent engineering guidance.
Enabling efficient engineering cognition.
Supporting knowledge transfer across engineering domains.
Maintaining traceability to its supporting foundations.
7. Normative Requirements

Engineering Knowledge SHALL:

Be derived from validated Engineering Understanding.
Maintain traceability to supporting Engineering Memory and Engineering Evidence.
Be reusable across applicable engineering contexts.
Preserve explanatory information regarding its origin and applicability.
Be revised when justified by significant new evidence.

Atlas SHALL NOT classify transient or unvalidated understanding as Engineering Knowledge.

8. Non-Goals

Engineering Knowledge does not:

Replace Engineering Reality.
Replace Engineering Observation.
Replace Engineering Evidence.
Replace current Engineering Understanding.
Eliminate the need for Engineering Reasoning.
Represent immutable engineering truth.
9. Future Evolution

Future revisions MAY introduce formal knowledge representation models, ontology mappings, inference structures, and domain-specific knowledge taxonomies without changing the canonical definition established by this specification.